# ncdurs example configuration
#
# Copy this file to ~/.config/ncdu/config (or to /etc/ncdu.conf for a
# system-wide one) and uncomment what you need. Everything in here is
# commented out on purpose: as it stands, the file changes nothing.
#
# Format (identical to the C version):
#   - one option per line; an option and its value are separated by spaces
#   - '#' starts a comment, blank lines are ignored
#   - a leading '@' makes the line tolerant: a file it refers to may be
#     missing without that being an error
#   - options given on the command line always win over this file
#
# The paths are, in this order: /etc/ncdu.conf, then
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ncdu/config or ~/.config/ncdu/config.

# --- scanning -------------------------------------------------------------

# Collect uid/gid/mode/mtime. Enables the mtime column ('m') and sorting by
# it ('M'), and makes the export carry those four fields.
# -e

# Leave matching entries out of the statistics. A pattern is matched against
# the whole path *and* every suffix of it, so ".git" also hits
# "/home/me/project/.git".
# --exclude .git
# --exclude node_modules
# --exclude *.iso

# Read further patterns from a file, one per line. The '@' means a missing
# file is not an error - handy for a personal exclude list.
# @--exclude-from ~/.ncduexcludes

# Do not cross filesystem boundaries; skip cache directories (those holding a
# CACHEDIR.TAG) and Linux pseudo filesystems such as /proc and /sys.
# -x
# --exclude-caches
# --exclude-kernfs

# Count a symlink to a file as the file it points at.
# -L

# --- interface ------------------------------------------------------------

# Colour scheme: auto (the default) uses the dark theme on colour-capable
# terminals and off otherwise. The C version has no 'auto'.
# --color auto
# --color dark

# Which columns and which sort order to start with.
# --show-itemcount
# --show-mtime
# --show-percent
# --graph-style eighth-block
# --sort name
# --group-directories-first

# --- safety ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Read-only mode: '-r' disables deletion, '-rr' the shell as well.
# -r

# Ask before quitting (deletion already asks by default).
# --confirm-quit

# Ignore this file entirely. Also available on the command line.
# --ignore-config
